"""
=========================================================================================
 ML 102 - DRUG ADOPTION CAPSTONE  |  END-TO-END PRODUCTION PIPELINE  (v2 - RECALL FOCUSED)
=========================================================================================
Objective : Predict which of the 1,502 non-adopted physicians (Test_physicians.csv) will
            adopt drug 'XYZ' in Quarter 11, using 10 quarters of historical transactional
            data (Input_data_file1.csv) and static physician profile data
            (Input_data_file2.csv).

DESIGN ASSUMPTIONS (unchanged from v1, documented for the reviewer):
  A1. A physician is only a valid "non-adopted" training example for a given target
      quarter Q if brand_prescribed == 0 for every quarter strictly before Q for that
      physician. Once a physician adopts, all subsequent quarters are dropped from the
      panel -> we only ever model "will a still-non-adopted physician adopt THIS quarter".
  A2. lag1 = most recent quarter strictly before the target quarter.
      lag2 = second most recent quarter strictly before the target quarter.
      Both built with groupby(physician_id).shift() -> the target quarter's own values
      are NEVER used as features -> zero leakage.
  A3. Rows where lag1 is completely unavailable (physician's very first observed quarter)
      carry no historical signal and are dropped. Rows where only lag2 is unavailable are
      kept; lag_avg / lag_change are left NaN for those rows.
  A4. For scoring Test_physicians.csv (Q11), lag1 = Quarter 10, lag2 = Quarter 9.
  A5. All EDA decisions (missingness drop, gender mode, VIF) are LEARNED ON TRAIN ONLY and
      simply APPLIED to test / the Q11 scoring set.

=========================================================================================
 WHAT CHANGED IN v2 AND WHY (read this before re-running)
=========================================================================================
Symptom in v1: test recall ~0.49-0.50, and only 8 of 1,502 physicians flagged as adopters
in the Q11 scoring output when the business expects roughly 200-300.

Root causes and fixes:

  C1. SMOTE REMOVED.
      SMOTE synthesizes fake minority-class rows that only resemble the *training*
      distribution. Both models were trained on ~50/50 synthetic-heavy data, then scored
      with a raw 0.5 threshold on REAL, ~90/10 data. The decision boundary learned on
      synthetic points did not transfer to real physicians -> collapsed recall on test,
      and near-total collapse on the Q11 panel (a single "last quarter" snapshot per
      physician, structurally different from the multi-row training panel).
      FIX: scale_pos_weight (computed from the TRUE class counts, per fold / per final
      fit) instead of resampling. The model trains on real data only, with
      well-calibrated probabilities.

  C2. TUNING OBJECTIVE CHANGED FROM MEAN ROC-AUC -> MEAN BEST-F2.
      ROC-AUC rewards ranking quality across the *entire* curve, which for a ~8-12%
      positive-rate problem is dominated by easy true negatives -> a model can score
      ROC-AUC = 0.87-0.94 while recall sits at 0.49. F-beta with beta=2 weights recall
      twice as heavily as precision, which matches the business cost structure here
      (missing a physician who is about to adopt is more costly than one extra follow-up
      call on a false positive). Optuna now tunes for mean best-F2 across the rolling
      time-based CV folds.

  C3. DECISION THRESHOLD IS NO LONGER HARDCODED AT 0.5.
      For each model, we pool out-of-fold predictions across the rolling-window CV splits
      (leak-free: every validation fold is scored by a model trained only on strictly
      earlier quarters, never on the target fold or on test) and pick the threshold that
      maximizes F2 on that pooled OOF set. That single threshold is then applied
      consistently to TRAIN, TEST, and the Q11 scoring panel -- no threshold is ever
      chosen by looking at test or Q11 results (that would be leakage).

  C4. MODEL SELECTION (XGBoost vs LightGBM) IS NOW BASED ON TEST F2, NOT TEST ROC-AUC,
      per your instruction to judge by the metric that matches the business situation
      (finding adopters), not by AUC.

  C5. Q11 OUTPUT NOW ALSO INCLUDES A RANK-BASED "TOP-N" FLAG.
      A single global probability threshold is inherently a bit noisy for a fixed-size
      business ask ("give the sales team ~250-300 names"). So alongside the
      threshold-based class, we also flag the top 20% of the 1,502 physicians by
      predicted probability, which is a stable way to hit a target list size regardless
      of small threshold shifts. Both columns are written to the scored output so you can
      pick whichever the business prefers.

Run order: unchanged - organized as STEP blocks. Adjust FILE PATHS in STEP 0 and run
top to bottom.
=========================================================================================
"""

